## Why This Is Asked

Explores an IaC-first approach and introduces resource isolation (raw vs sanitized) plus BigQuery integration. Tests comprehension of permissions, idempotency, and end-to-end flow.

## Key Concepts

- Terraform modules and provider configuration
- Pub/Sub triggers for Cloud Functions
- GCS bucket versioning and least-privilege IAM
- JSON validation and regex-based redaction
- BigQuery insert with insertId for idempotency
- Basic end-to-end test plan

## Code Example

```javascript
// telemetry-processor/index.js
const {Storage} = require('@google-cloud/storage');
const {BigQuery} = require('@google-cloud/bigquery');
const storage = new Storage();
const bigquery = new BigQuery();
const sanitizedBucket = process.env.SANITIZED_BUCKET;

exports.telemetryProcessor = async (event, context) => {
  const raw = Buffer.from(event.data, 'base64').toString();
  let obj = JSON.parse(raw);
  // redact common PII
  ['ssn','email','phone','card'].forEach(k => { if (obj[k]) obj[k] = 'REDACTED'; });
  const bucket = storage.bucket(sanitizedBucket);
  const name = `${obj.id || context.eventId}.json`;
  await bucket.file(name).save(JSON.stringify(obj));
  // BigQuery insert (pseudo): await bigquery.insert({ ... }, { insertId: `${name}` });
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test idempotency with duplicate Pub/Sub messages?
- How would you extend this to schema evolution and monitoring without breaking existing inserts?

